          Calculating precise chronological age involves far more than dividing total elapsed days by 365 or 30.4375. The modern Gregorian calendar features variable month lengths ranging from 28 to 31 days and inserts leap years every four years—except for century years not evenly divisible by 400 (for example, the year 2000 was a leap year, while 1900 and 2100 are not).
        </p>
        <p>
          To calculate your age accurately by date of birth without rounding drift, the engine first tallies completed whole calendar years between your birth year and the target date. Next, it computes completed whole months. If the day of the target month is smaller than your birth day, the algorithm "borrows" the exact number of days from the immediately preceding calendar month (e.g., 31 days from July or 28/29 days from February).
        </p>
        <p>
          Furthermore, all temporal math executes using UTC-standardized timestamps. This guarantees that regional Daylight Saving Time (DST) transitions never cause an accidental one-hour shift that flips your calculated birthday by an entire calendar day. If you want to uncover the weekday you were born or your astrological traits, visit our <a href="/birthday-facts" class="text-accent hover:underline font-medium">birthday facts dossier</a>, or set a live milestone alert on our <a href="/countdown" class="text-accent hover:underline font-medium">event countdown timer</a>.
